###

### Test Invalid File Format (Should Return 400)
POST http://localhost:8080/api/v1/qr/scan
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=----FormBoundary7MA4YWxkTrZu0gW

------FormBoundary7MA4YWxkTrZu0gW
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="document.txt"
Content-Type: text/plain

This is a text file, not an image
------FormBoundary7MA4YWxkTrZu0gW--

###

### Success Response Example:
# Status: 200 OK
# Body:
{
  "qrData": "https://example.com/payment?id=12345&amount=500000"
}

###

### Error Response Example - Invalid Format:
# Status: 400 Bad Request
# Body:
Invalid file format. Only PNG, JPG, JPEG are allowed

###

### Error Response Example - Empty File:
# Status: 400 Bad Request
# Body:
File is required and cannot be empty

###

### Error Response Example - No QR Code:
# Status: 400 Bad Request
# Body:
No QR code found in the image

###

### Using cURL for Testing:

# Test with PNG:
curl -X POST http://localhost:8080/api/v1/qr/scan \
  -F "file=@qr_code.png"

# Test with JPG:
curl -X POST http://localhost:8080/api/v1/qr/scan \
  -F "file=@qr_code.jpg"

# Expected successful response:
# {"qrData":"decoded_qr_string_here"}

###

### Using Postman (GUI):

1. Open Postman
2. Create New Request: POST
3. URL: http://localhost:8080/api/v1/qr/scan
4. Go to "Body" tab
5. Select "form-data"
6. Add key: "file" (type: File)
7. Choose PNG/JPG/JPEG image
8. Click Send
9. Should get response with decoded QR data

###

### Headers Automatically Set:
# - Content-Type: multipart/form-data (auto by multipart)
# - No authentication required (uses Spring default security)

###

